<section class="fcd-section" id="gaps">
    <h2>Security gaps</h2>
    <h3>Fixed (October 2026)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>ChurchSuite account takeover</strong>: the connect routes (<code>/churchsuite/authorize</code>, <code>check-auth</code>, <code>/ChurchSuite/Authorize</code>, <code>Callback</code>) now require sign-in and act only for the signed-in user. A user ID from the query string, session or OAuth challenge must match them and is never used to sign anyone in. Redirects after ChurchSuite are limited to this site.</li>
        <li><strong>Live log feed</strong>: <code>/logsHub</code> requires <code>Admin.Read</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Secrets in the repository</strong>: removed from the current files (Azure client secret, database password, Google key, the email app's JWT key, development Data Protection keys, an unused <code>map.js</code> that embedded the Google key). Development secrets now come from <code>dotnet user-secrets</code>; <code>deploy.yml</code> reads <code>AZURE_CREDENTIALS</code>. They're still in Git history, so <strong>they must be rotated</strong> (see <a href="/docs/system/operations#rotate-exposed">rotating the exposed secrets</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>ChurchSuite tokens and API keys</strong> are encrypted at rest (see <a href="/docs/system/data#encryption">Data &amp; encryption</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>Account lockout</strong>: 5 attempts, 15 minutes. Sign-in also now honours 2FA.</li>
        <li><strong>Content Security Policy</strong> is sent on every page.</li>
        <li><strong>Google key</strong> is no longer returned to browsers.</li>
        <li><strong>Email content</strong>: everything people type is HTML-encoded before it goes into an email.</li>
        <li><code>/oauth-debug</code> is Development-only.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Still open</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Rotate the exposed secrets and purge Git history.</strong> Until then, anyone with a copy of the repository has working credentials.</li>
        <li><strong>Registration needs only a whitelisted address</strong>, not the token. See <a href="/docs/system/accounts#whitelist">Accounts</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>No IP rate limiting</strong> on sign-in, the contact form or search.</li>
        <li><strong>Email app</strong>: Swagger UI is public; SMTP uses STARTTLS <em>when available</em>, so it would fall back to plain text if the server didn't offer TLS; the <code>Template</code> field is a file path chosen by the caller (only a valid-JWT caller, so low risk).</li>
        <li><strong>Restrict the Google API key</strong> in Google Cloud to the Geocoding and Places APIs and the site's outbound IP.</li>
    </ol>
</section>
